1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,740
How does a hundred-metre tree drink with no pump?

2
00:00:04,060 --> 00:00:05,828
An ordinary suction pump can

3
00:00:05,678 --> 00:00:07,770
only lift water about ten metres.

4
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:11,690
Yet redwoods grow past a hundred and ten metres.

5
00:00:11,888 --> 00:00:13,698
The trick starts at the top.

6
00:00:14,145 --> 00:00:16,585
Sun evaporates water from the leaves, and

7
00:00:16,435 --> 00:00:18,875
each lost molecule tugs on the next.

8
00:00:19,278 --> 00:00:21,357
Thin tubes called xylem hold unbroken

9
00:00:21,207 --> 00:00:23,608
threads of water, down to the roots.

10
00:00:23,917 --> 00:00:25,527
So the water isn't pushed.

11
00:00:25,650 --> 00:00:28,180
It's pulled, under negative pressure.

12
00:00:28,233 --> 00:00:29,903
Literally stretched.

13
00:00:30,382 --> 00:00:34,042
In 1965, scientists measured it: about minus five

14
00:00:33,892 --> 00:00:37,552
atmospheres in damp forests, minus eighty in deserts.

15
00:00:37,953 --> 00:00:40,553
In 2008, engineers built a gel synthetic

16
00:00:40,403 --> 00:00:43,003
tree that pulls water the same way.

17
00:00:43,179 --> 00:00:45,269
But stretched water is fragile.

18
00:00:45,486 --> 00:00:47,566
In severe drought, air bubbles snap

19
00:00:47,416 --> 00:00:49,496
the threads, and trees can die.

20
00:00:50,204 --> 00:00:52,434
Every tree you pass is lifting

21
00:00:52,284 --> 00:00:54,514
stretched water, pulled by the sun.
